1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:04,261
Why can the same probability landscape
describe two different dynamical regimes?

2
00:00:04,882 --> 00:00:06,702
And when does learning improve a decision?

3
00:00:07,263 --> 00:00:09,604
This graphical abstract gives us a working
map.

4
00:00:09,964 --> 00:00:13,085
At the top, compare stationary currents
over the same density.

5
00:00:13,485 --> 00:00:18,007
In the middle, follow observation,
inference, policy evaluation and

6
00:00:18,187 --> 00:00:18,487
action.

7
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:23,069
At the bottom, keep specification,
computation and proof distinct.

8
00:00:23,569 --> 00:00:28,011
This is the revised Synthetic manuscript;
the linked release contains this paper

9
00:00:28,111 --> 00:00:31,632
and lecture, while Zenodo preserves the
earlier published edition.

10
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:35,874
In twenty minutes we will make these
connections executable, using tested

11
00:00:35,934 --> 00:00:40,215
numerical models, selected Lean results
and concrete model specifications.

12
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:42,536
Read every result with its assumptions.

13
00:00:45,818 --> 00:00:47,119
Keep three questions in view.

14
00:00:47,299 --> 00:00:48,539
What explains an observation?

15
00:00:48,779 --> 00:00:51,260
A posterior belief answers that inference
question.

16
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:52,741
Which action should come next?

17
00:00:53,041 --> 00:00:56,462
Expected free energy compares predicted
outcomes using preferences and

18
00:00:56,482 --> 00:00:57,363
information gain.

19
00:00:57,763 --> 00:00:59,463
What maintains a stationary distribution?

20
00:00:59,744 --> 00:01:03,105
Drift, diffusion, current, and entropy
production describe that dynamical

21
00:01:03,145 --> 00:01:03,485
regime.

22
00:01:03,745 --> 00:01:07,607
Variational and expected free energy are
information theoretic quantities.

23
00:01:07,807 --> 00:01:10,828
Physical free energy needs a thermodynamic
model and units.

24
00:01:11,188 --> 00:01:14,109
The shared vocabulary does not make these
objects interchangeable.

25
00:01:14,569 --> 00:01:17,791
As you read the three cards, connect each
question to its mathematical

26
00:01:17,871 --> 00:01:18,211
object.

27
00:01:21,553 --> 00:01:23,534
Start with a hidden state and an
observation.

28
00:01:23,754 --> 00:01:27,176
A prior describes uncertainty about the
hidden state before the observation.

29
00:01:27,737 --> 00:01:31,279
A likelihood describes which observations
each state tends to generate.

30
00:01:31,779 --> 00:01:34,981
Multiplying prior and likelihood produces
the joint distribution.

31
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:38,563
Summing over hidden states gives the
evidence for the observed outcome.

32
00:01:38,943 --> 00:01:42,945
Bayes' rule then divides the appropriate
joint probabilities by that evidence.

33
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:47,288
In the repository's finite models,
conditional distributions are stored in

34
00:01:47,348 --> 00:01:49,809
columns, so each column must sum to one.

35
00:01:50,149 --> 00:01:54,952
This orientation matters: transposing a
plausible looking matrix can change the

36
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:55,713
entire model.

37
00:01:56,313 --> 00:02:01,096
The examples reject invalid probabilities
and impossible observations, instead

38
00:02:01,136 --> 00:02:03,677
of silently replacing them with an
arbitrary belief.

39
00:02:06,999 --> 00:02:09,219
Now introduce a candidate belief, called
q.

40
00:02:09,399 --> 00:02:13,120
Variational free energy compares this
belief with the generative model after an

41
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,280
observation has arrived.

42
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:19,161
One decomposition is complexity minus
accuracy: divergence from the prior, minus

43
00:02:19,221 --> 00:02:20,321
expected log likelihood.

44
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:22,541
A second decomposition is especially
revealing.

45
00:02:22,821 --> 00:02:26,522
Free energy equals the observation's
surprise plus the divergence between the

46
00:02:26,562 --> 00:02:28,522
candidate belief and the exact posterior.

47
00:02:28,942 --> 00:02:32,503
Because this divergence is nonnegative on
the stated support domain, free energy

48
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:33,703
bounds surprise from above.

49
00:02:34,103 --> 00:02:37,083
When the candidate belief equals the
posterior, the gap closes.

50
00:02:37,483 --> 00:02:38,983
This is an identity within the model.

51
00:02:39,283 --> 00:02:42,664
It does not say that every physical system
computes Bayes' rule, or

52
00:02:42,724 --> 00:02:45,444
that minimizing an arbitrary scalar makes
a system intelligent.

53
00:02:48,546 --> 00:02:50,646
Read the belief figure from left to right.

54
00:02:51,446 --> 00:02:55,227
The categorical belief moves from a
uniform assignment toward the exact

55
00:02:55,287 --> 00:02:55,887
posterior.

56
00:02:56,687 --> 00:02:59,087
Free energy approaches the surprise
reference line.

57
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:01,388
The gap is posterior divergence.

58
00:03:02,168 --> 00:03:06,728
Check the answer independently using
Bayes' rule and the complexity minus

59
00:03:06,868 --> 00:03:08,209
accuracy calculation.

60
00:03:09,129 --> 00:03:11,769
Their agreement supplies a numerical
oracle.

61
00:03:12,809 --> 00:03:16,090
The descent algorithm needs safeguards and
convergence checks.

62
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:19,750
A lower bound by itself cannot guarantee
convergence.

63
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:22,971
Check the updates against the exact
posterior.

64
00:03:23,471 --> 00:03:27,091
The changing belief and the shrinking gap
should tell the same story.

65
00:03:30,293 --> 00:03:33,974
We now switch from a discrete belief to a
continuous, two dimensional

66
00:03:34,014 --> 00:03:34,614
model state.

67
00:03:34,914 --> 00:03:39,274
The Gaussian contours define a
dimensionless stationary potential, Phi.

68
00:03:39,634 --> 00:03:43,395
This is a function of coordinates,
distinct from the variational functional

69
00:03:43,535 --> 00:03:44,855
of a recognition distribution.

70
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:48,976
The drift is Q minus Gamma, acting on the
gradient of Phi.

71
00:03:49,616 --> 00:03:54,217
Gamma supplies dissipation; antisymmetric
Q supplies tangential motion.

72
00:03:54,877 --> 00:03:58,537
Along the noise-free drift, the potential
decreases because the gradient is

73
00:03:58,637 --> 00:04:00,658
orthogonal to its solenoidal component.

74
00:04:01,058 --> 00:04:05,238
That geometric identity does not mean
circulation has zero physical cost.

75
00:04:05,859 --> 00:04:09,779
Connecting these coordinates to beliefs,
or scaling this potential to energy in

76
00:04:09,819 --> 00:04:11,959
joules, requires additional assumptions.

77
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:13,500
Keep those bridges visible.

78
00:04:16,742 --> 00:04:19,523
The trajectory figure integrates those
drift fields.

79
00:04:20,043 --> 00:04:24,264
With no solenoidal term, the path relaxes
radially toward the mode.

80
00:04:24,704 --> 00:04:29,505
With circulation, the path spirals inward
while the landscape still decreases.

81
00:04:30,006 --> 00:04:34,287
The numerical integrator is a fixed step
fourth order Runge Kutta method,

82
00:04:34,587 --> 00:04:37,147
checked against the available linear
closed form.

83
00:04:37,628 --> 00:04:39,728
Two cautions make the picture more useful.

84
00:04:40,088 --> 00:04:44,729
First, total rotation depends on the
chosen time window; angular velocity is

85
00:04:44,789 --> 00:04:48,050
the more intrinsic diagnostic in this
isotropic example.

86
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:52,791
Second, both deterministic trajectories
relax toward the mode.

87
00:04:53,252 --> 00:04:56,552
The spiral does not depict a particle
moving forever on a stable

88
00:04:56,652 --> 00:04:56,953
orbit.

89
00:04:57,713 --> 00:05:01,974
A non-equilibrium steady state is an
ensemble property of the corresponding

90
00:05:02,014 --> 00:05:04,054
stochastic model, which we examine next.

91
00:05:07,416 --> 00:05:09,476
Add matched diffusion to the linear drift.

92
00:05:09,997 --> 00:05:13,417
The stationary covariance is inverse
precision in both regimes.

93
00:05:14,057 --> 00:05:17,818
To see a transient, begin with that
covariance but displace the Gaussian

94
00:05:17,858 --> 00:05:18,098
mean.

95
00:05:18,678 --> 00:05:23,319
The contour cloud now changes position:
its mean follows the relaxing drift,

96
00:05:23,579 --> 00:05:26,100
radially on the left and spirally on the
right.

97
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:29,780
The clock is model time, not an inference
iteration.

98
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:33,981
This moving density is not stationary,
even though the coefficients are

99
00:05:34,081 --> 00:05:34,601
constant.

100
00:05:35,121 --> 00:05:39,922
Individual noisy paths would fluctuate;
these contours show the ensemble

101
00:05:39,982 --> 00:05:40,642
distribution.

102
00:05:41,283 --> 00:05:44,483
A stationary density instead stops
changing with time.

103
00:05:44,903 --> 00:05:47,504
It may nevertheless support a probability
current.

104
00:05:48,124 --> 00:05:50,984
The next animation isolates that different
question.

105
00:05:54,307 --> 00:05:58,229
Here every frame is a stationary model
with the same Gaussian density.

106
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:02,552
We sweep a known drive parameter; this
clock is not physical time.

107
00:06:03,092 --> 00:06:07,034
The arrows are probability current, not
individual particle trajectories.

108
00:06:07,674 --> 00:06:11,717
At zero drive the current vanishes
everywhere and detailed balance holds in

109
00:06:11,757 --> 00:06:13,177
this even-coordinate model.

110
00:06:13,397 --> 00:06:16,719
On either side, the arrows circulate with
zero divergence.

111
00:06:17,119 --> 00:06:21,262
Reversing drive reverses circulation while
preserving the density, and the

112
00:06:21,362 --> 00:06:23,903
entropy-production curve depends on its
square.

113
00:06:24,363 --> 00:06:27,985
Notice that current vanishes at the mode
even in the driven regime.

114
00:06:28,686 --> 00:06:31,867
One zero arrow therefore cannot establish
equilibrium.

115
00:06:32,408 --> 00:06:36,750
A histogram sees the shared density;
temporal and reversal statistics

116
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:38,251
distinguish the processes.

117
00:06:41,493 --> 00:06:45,653
Entropy production measures the difference
between forward and reversed path

118
00:06:45,673 --> 00:06:46,274
statistics.

119
00:06:46,554 --> 00:06:50,474
For this elliptic, stationary diffusion
with even coordinates, the current

120
00:06:50,514 --> 00:06:54,815
formula gives twice squared drive times
precision, divided by dissipation.

121
00:06:55,035 --> 00:06:57,835
Positive precision and dissipation are
essential.

122
00:06:58,095 --> 00:07:02,175
Momentum variables, a reversed magnetic
field or a different reverse protocol

123
00:07:02,295 --> 00:07:06,136
require a separate analysis; a circulating
phase-space current is not

124
00:07:06,236 --> 00:07:07,396
universally dissipative.

125
00:07:07,616 --> 00:07:12,297
Nonzero drive establishes non-equilibrium
here, not automatically far from

126
00:07:12,377 --> 00:07:12,897
equilibrium.

127
00:07:13,197 --> 00:07:16,997
Scaling the informational rate to entropy
or power requires temperature,

128
00:07:17,297 --> 00:07:19,877
Boltzmann's constant and a compatible
physical model.

129
00:07:20,358 --> 00:07:24,398
This simulation does not measure
metabolism or a computer's energy budget.

130
00:07:24,858 --> 00:07:29,219
The paper cites stationary diffusion
theory and stochastic thermodynamics for

131
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:30,059
these distinctions.

132
00:07:33,301 --> 00:07:36,102
We now ask how a model separates a system
from its surroundings.

133
00:07:36,422 --> 00:07:41,183
The Markov blanket example partitions
variables into external, sensory, active,

134
00:07:41,383 --> 00:07:42,223
and internal roles.

135
00:07:42,724 --> 00:07:46,825
The defining statistical condition is that
external and internal states become

136
00:07:46,885 --> 00:07:49,425
independent after conditioning on the
blanket variables.

137
00:07:49,906 --> 00:07:53,887
This allows marginal dependence and
conditional independence to coexist.

138
00:07:54,327 --> 00:07:58,448
The figure makes that contrast visible:
ordinary correlations can connect

139
00:07:58,728 --> 00:08:02,629
external and internal variables, while a
particular entry of the Gaussian

140
00:08:02,669 --> 00:08:04,550
precision matrix is zero.

141
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:09,511
For a nonsingular Gaussian distribution,
that zero identifies conditional

142
00:08:09,591 --> 00:08:11,632
independence given the remaining
variables.

143
00:08:12,452 --> 00:08:16,193
The model therefore demonstrates a precise
screening relation, rather than a

144
00:08:16,233 --> 00:08:19,254
literal wall separating two independent
parts of the world.

145
00:08:22,596 --> 00:08:25,999
Statistical screening alone does not
assign causal direction or prove that

146
00:08:26,059 --> 00:08:28,101
internal states represent external states.

147
00:08:28,501 --> 00:08:31,964
Those claims require additional dynamical
or interventional assumptions.

148
00:08:32,584 --> 00:08:36,347
Here we can establish something precise:
conditioning on the full blanket

149
00:08:36,447 --> 00:08:38,529
removes the relevant Gaussian dependence.

150
00:08:39,009 --> 00:08:41,431
Conditioning on only part need not do so.

151
00:08:41,932 --> 00:08:44,994
Compare the marginal and conditional
panels before moving on.

152
00:08:45,455 --> 00:08:49,157
A blanket is useful because the
conditioning statement can be tested; it

153
00:08:49,218 --> 00:08:51,920
is not a literal wall or a certificate of
cognition.

154
00:08:55,142 --> 00:08:59,364
The revised blanket figure also separates
a canonical Gaussian model selected

155
00:08:59,424 --> 00:09:00,785
for its formal correspondence.

156
00:09:01,205 --> 00:09:04,807
Its precision matrix has four on the
diagonal, negative one on the

157
00:09:04,847 --> 00:09:08,849
blanket connections, and zero direct
external to internal coupling.

158
00:09:09,410 --> 00:09:12,451
This is a distinct model from the original
numerical example.

159
00:09:12,871 --> 00:09:16,793
The added panel makes the distinction
explicit instead of silently transferring

160
00:09:16,833 --> 00:09:18,514
a theorem between different matrices.

161
00:09:19,095 --> 00:09:22,817
Conditional Gaussian calculations expose
the screened relation, while a

162
00:09:22,857 --> 00:09:25,538
perturbed direct connection supplies a
counterexample.

163
00:09:25,958 --> 00:09:29,740
Matching a concrete probability model to a
formal statement requires this level

164
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:34,363
of specificity: variable order, precision
entries, conditioning variables, and

165
00:09:34,403 --> 00:09:35,843
the exact property being claimed.

166
00:09:39,145 --> 00:09:41,746
Perception evaluates beliefs after an
observation.

167
00:09:42,166 --> 00:09:45,908
Decision making evaluates possible
observations before they arrive.

168
00:09:46,488 --> 00:09:48,689
Each candidate action has an outcome
model.

169
00:09:49,089 --> 00:09:51,910
Combine that model with the present belief
to predict outcomes.

170
00:09:52,370 --> 00:09:55,072
Then score the action using expected free
energy.

171
00:09:55,352 --> 00:09:59,713
Our score is negative pragmatic value
minus epistemic value.

172
00:10:00,154 --> 00:10:03,775
The pragmatic term averages log
preferences over predicted outcomes.

173
00:10:04,215 --> 00:10:07,897
The epistemic term measures expected
information gain about hidden states.

174
00:10:08,337 --> 00:10:10,538
These are distinct contributions to one
objective.

175
00:10:10,958 --> 00:10:13,739
Lower expected free energy is preferred.

176
00:10:14,079 --> 00:10:17,681
The chosen likelihoods, preferences and
available actions determine the

177
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:18,601
resulting behavior.

178
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:21,682
Read the two terms separately before
comparing the bars.

179
00:10:24,903 --> 00:10:28,664
The one step example compares an
informative cue with an uninformative bet.

180
00:10:29,144 --> 00:10:33,585
With flat preferences, pragmatic values
are equal, so the cue wins through

181
00:10:33,685 --> 00:10:34,526
information gain.

182
00:10:35,066 --> 00:10:37,767
Concentrate preferences enough, and the
rewarding bet wins.

183
00:10:38,467 --> 00:10:41,988
The same score changes the balance without
a separate exploration switch.

184
00:10:42,508 --> 00:10:47,029
These supplied matrices expose a
mechanism, rather than explaining every

185
00:10:47,209 --> 00:10:48,210
intelligent action.

186
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:52,731
Before leaving the figure, identify which
bar changes when preferences change

187
00:10:52,911 --> 00:10:54,931
and which term describes information.

188
00:10:58,193 --> 00:11:02,814
To understand the epistemic term, imagine
following each possible observation

189
00:11:02,934 --> 00:11:03,394
separately.

190
00:11:04,194 --> 00:11:08,015
For each observation, update the prior
belief using Bayes' rule.

191
00:11:08,695 --> 00:11:13,096
Measure how far that posterior moves from
the prior using relative entropy.

192
00:11:13,856 --> 00:11:17,837
Then average those changes with the
predicted observation probabilities.

193
00:11:18,497 --> 00:11:23,059
This is the expected information gain,
equal here to mutual information between

194
00:11:23,099 --> 00:11:24,739
the hidden state and the outcome.

195
00:11:25,439 --> 00:11:29,880
An observation independent of the hidden
state leaves the posterior unchanged

196
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:32,241
and contributes zero information.

197
00:11:32,821 --> 00:11:37,162
An informative observation can contribute
positive information even before we

198
00:11:37,202 --> 00:11:38,822
know which outcome will occur.

199
00:11:39,362 --> 00:11:43,443
Notice the support conditions: impossible
observations cannot be treated as

200
00:11:43,643 --> 00:11:47,884
ordinary posterior updates, and a
logarithm involving zero probability

201
00:11:48,024 --> 00:11:49,844
requires an explicit convention.

202
00:11:53,125 --> 00:11:56,566
The planning example extends actions into
short, fixed sequences.

203
00:11:57,086 --> 00:12:00,687
Each policy induces a distribution over
observation sequences.

204
00:12:01,267 --> 00:12:05,168
Follow every possible branch, update the
posterior along that branch, and

205
00:12:05,288 --> 00:12:07,029
average the evaluated contributions.

206
00:12:07,669 --> 00:12:10,089
The pragmatic term concerns the terminal
outcome.

207
00:12:10,549 --> 00:12:14,971
The epistemic term scores information
across the whole observation sequence.

208
00:12:15,491 --> 00:12:17,571
This is one specific planning objective.

209
00:12:18,231 --> 00:12:21,252
Other active inference models may use
different objectives.

210
00:12:21,812 --> 00:12:26,354
Here the horizon and outcome spaces are
small, so we can enumerate

211
00:12:26,394 --> 00:12:27,674
branches exactly.

212
00:12:28,394 --> 00:12:30,155
There is no sampling uncertainty.

213
00:12:30,675 --> 00:12:32,995
Longer horizons grow combinatorially.

214
00:12:33,455 --> 00:12:38,437
This exact calculation is an explanatory
reference, not a claim of scalable

215
00:12:38,517 --> 00:12:38,857
planning.

216
00:12:42,078 --> 00:12:45,259
Information gain and decision value answer
different questions.

217
00:12:45,459 --> 00:12:50,140
Consider two equally likely states and two
commitments, each paying one utility

218
00:12:50,220 --> 00:12:51,981
unit only when it matches the state.

219
00:12:52,161 --> 00:12:55,062
Without a cue, the best expected payoff is
one half.

220
00:12:55,322 --> 00:12:59,403
A ninety percent reliable cue followed by
the matching commitment raises

221
00:12:59,583 --> 00:13:01,504
expected payoff to point nine.

222
00:13:01,704 --> 00:13:05,805
The gross value of information is point
four; subtracting a point fifteen

223
00:13:05,925 --> 00:13:08,346
observation cost leaves point two five.

224
00:13:08,586 --> 00:13:13,007
Watch the cue accuracy sweep: decision
value changes in utility units,

225
00:13:13,427 --> 00:13:15,888
while mutual information changes in nats.

226
00:13:16,548 --> 00:13:20,489
An informative cue can have zero decision
value when payoffs do not

227
00:13:20,529 --> 00:13:21,910
depend on the hidden state.

228
00:13:22,570 --> 00:13:27,271
Exhaustive enumeration of all finite
feedback policies independently checks the

229
00:13:27,291 --> 00:13:27,972
calculation.

230
00:13:28,392 --> 00:13:32,813
This comparison assumes a fixed state,
known likelihood and utilities,

231
00:13:33,273 --> 00:13:35,394
and unchanged available actions.

232
00:13:35,994 --> 00:13:39,915
Next we connect such contingent
commitments to the perception-action loop.

233
00:13:43,096 --> 00:13:46,597
The synthetic agent demonstration makes
actions and outcomes sequential.

234
00:13:46,997 --> 00:13:50,198
Initially, the agent is uncertain which
hidden context holds.

235
00:13:50,618 --> 00:13:52,878
It can obtain a cue or choose a commitment
action.

236
00:13:53,438 --> 00:13:57,199
After selecting an initial action, it
receives one explicitly specified

237
00:13:57,279 --> 00:14:00,180
observation and updates its belief by
Bayes' rule.

238
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:04,261
It then evaluates the available commitment
actions again under the posterior.

239
00:14:04,381 --> 00:14:08,182
The environment gives those commitments
state dependent consequences: choosing

240
00:14:08,202 --> 00:14:11,923
the action that matches the hidden context
produces reward, while choosing the

241
00:14:11,963 --> 00:14:13,103
other produces loss.

242
00:14:13,643 --> 00:14:17,224
This creates a setting in which
information can change the next decision.

243
00:14:17,724 --> 00:14:20,205
The small example is deliberately
transparent.

244
00:14:20,485 --> 00:14:23,965
It is a two stage model with supplied
probabilities, not a trained

245
00:14:24,025 --> 00:14:25,646
general purpose autonomous system.

246
00:14:28,967 --> 00:14:32,348
The informative cue identifies the hidden
context, allowing different

247
00:14:32,408 --> 00:14:34,789
observations to lead to different
commitment decisions.

248
00:14:35,389 --> 00:14:38,591
Compare that with a cue replaced by a fair
coin independent of

249
00:14:38,651 --> 00:14:39,211
context.

250
00:14:39,911 --> 00:14:43,492
The coin still generates observations, but
those observations carry no

251
00:14:43,532 --> 00:14:44,893
information about the hidden state.

252
00:14:45,393 --> 00:14:49,315
The posterior consequently stays at the
prior, and any change in behavior

253
00:14:49,375 --> 00:14:51,495
cannot be credited to learning the
context.

254
00:14:52,096 --> 00:14:56,177
This comparison separates receiving a
signal from receiving useful evidence.

255
00:14:56,577 --> 00:15:00,819
It also separates rerunning a decision
rule from genuinely observation dependent

256
00:15:00,839 --> 00:15:01,279
behavior.

257
00:15:01,859 --> 00:15:06,101
The repository represents branches
explicitly, making both the informative

258
00:15:06,161 --> 00:15:08,682
case and its counterexample reproducible.

259
00:15:09,362 --> 00:15:12,803
Neither the selected branch nor its clean
outcome should be mistaken for

260
00:15:12,843 --> 00:15:15,704
a population estimate of real world agent
performance.

261
00:15:19,005 --> 00:15:22,046
The hierarchy example adds a second hidden
level.

262
00:15:22,306 --> 00:15:26,328
Context influences a lower level state,
and that state influences an

263
00:15:26,488 --> 00:15:27,128
observation.

264
00:15:27,428 --> 00:15:31,270
Top down prediction starts with the
context prior, propagates it through the

265
00:15:31,310 --> 00:15:35,852
state given context distribution, and then
through the observation given state

266
00:15:35,952 --> 00:15:36,512
distribution.

267
00:15:37,232 --> 00:15:40,934
Bottom up inference conditions the full
joint distribution on an actual

268
00:15:41,014 --> 00:15:41,554
observation.

269
00:15:42,454 --> 00:15:46,956
Marginalizing the resulting posterior
joint gives revised beliefs at both hidden

270
00:15:46,996 --> 00:15:47,316
levels.

271
00:15:47,896 --> 00:15:51,618
This is exact finite Bayesian
conditioning, so the model provides an

272
00:15:51,678 --> 00:15:54,859
unusually clear reference for what a
hierarchical update means.

273
00:15:55,579 --> 00:15:58,921
The hierarchy is about the factorization
of a probability model.

274
00:15:59,521 --> 00:16:03,222
It does not require us to assume a
particular neural architecture, predictive

275
00:16:03,262 --> 00:16:05,423
coding implementation, or learning
algorithm.

276
00:16:08,644 --> 00:16:12,547
A lower level observation can revise
higher level context through the

277
00:16:12,587 --> 00:16:13,387
conditional model.

278
00:16:13,847 --> 00:16:18,070
If either connecting channel is
uninformative, evidence cannot recover the

279
00:16:18,090 --> 00:16:18,670
missing link.

280
00:16:19,191 --> 00:16:22,933
Direct enumeration of the joint
distribution independently checks the

281
00:16:22,973 --> 00:16:24,114
posterior marginals.

282
00:16:24,874 --> 00:16:29,257
This validates finite conditioning, not a
biological implementation, learned

283
00:16:29,297 --> 00:16:32,279
parameters, or an automatic benefit from
greater depth.

284
00:16:32,939 --> 00:16:36,941
Trace the figure downward for prediction,
